seniors=f'''<section class="hero"><div class="wrap hero-grid"><div class="hero-copy"><span class="eyebrow">For older adults &amp; the people who care</span><h1>Your technology.<br><em>Your confidence.</em></h1><p class="lead">Patient help with safer accounts, suspicious calls and everyday online decisions. We explain the options, work at your pace and keep you in control.</p><div class="actions">{button()}</div><p class="hero-note">At home in the Charlotte area · No obligation</p></div>{stage(True)}</div></section><section class="section divider"><div class="wrap split"><div><span class="eyebrow">Independence comes first</span><h2>Online-safety support.<br>On your terms.</h2><p style="margin-top:24px">You choose what changes, who receives information and whether a family member is involved. A relative can help arrange or pay for a visit; your permission still matters.</p></div><ul class="list"><li><strong>Accounts you can recover</strong><p>Review recovery options and appropriate passkeys or two-step verification. You enter credentials yourself.</p></li><li><strong>Calls and messages with less pressure</strong><p>Review supported spam controls without losing important calls. Practice a simple known-number callback routine.</p></li><li><strong>A trusted-contact plan</strong><p>Choose who can help and what they may see. Use explicit vendor invitations where available, not shared passwords.</p></li><li><strong>Instructions that stay with you</strong><p>For agreed setup work, a readable handoff with changes, remaining limitations and next steps you can practice.</p></li></ul></div></section><section class="section ink"><div class="wrap split"><div><span class="eyebrow">A habit worth keeping</span><h2>Pause.<br>Check.<br>Call back.</h2></div><div><p class="quote">An urgent message is a reason to slow down—not hand over access.</p><p style="margin-top:24px">End the call or message. Contact the person or organization through a number you already trust, such as the number on your bank card. Caller ID and a familiar-sounding voice are not proof.</p><a class="text-link" style="color:#add2ff" href="/blog/senior-scam-protection.html">Read the practical scam-response guide →</a></div></div></section>{process()}<section class="section"><div class="wrap faq"><h2>A useful starting point.<br>Not a guarantee.</h2><p>We can discuss customer-directed bank alerts, free credit freezes and optional consent-based monitoring. We don’t take custody of financial accounts, provide financial or legal advice, or promise that software will prevent every scam.</p><p>If a scam is happening now, contact your bank or payment provider immediately using a known number. This assessment request is not emergency support.</p></div></section>{cta()}'''
page('seniors.html','Online-safety help for older adults in Charlotte','Patient, consent-led help with account security, scam awareness and everyday technology. Free onsite assessment; implementation quoted separately.',seniors)

page('ThankYou.html','Your next step','An assessment request is not a confirmed appointment. Contact TechDad CLT with scheduling questions.','''<div class="wrap success"><span class="eyebrow">TechDad CLT / assessment request</span><h1 id="thanks-title">Let’s take the next step.</h1><p id="thanks-message">If you just submitted your request, we’ll contact you to discuss fit and scheduling. Your appointment is not booked yet. If you haven’t contacted us, use the assessment form to get started.</p><p>We’ll review your request and contact you to discuss fit and scheduling. There’s no need to send passwords, codes or sensitive records.</p><p>Questions? <a href="tel:+19803996020">(980) 399-6020</a> · <a href="mailto:hello@ampitsolutions.com">hello@ampitsolutions.com</a></p><div class="actions">'''+button('Back to TechDad','/',True)+'''</div></div>''',True)

