article('parent-guide.html','A practical parent’s guide to safer devices','Start with built-in tools. Add a paid product only for a specific, supported gap. Keep conversations part of the plan.','Parents / practical guide','''<h2>1. Start with the child’s device</h2><p>For Apple devices, review Family Sharing and Screen Time. For supported Android and Chromebook child devices, use Google Family Link. For Windows and Xbox, review Microsoft Family Safety. A parent app on an iPhone does not mean it can enforce controls on a child’s iPhone.</p><p>Native controls already include activity reports and selected notifications. They are not comprehensive message-risk monitoring, but they are not “zero visibility,” either.</p><h2>2. Agree on a manageable baseline</h2><ul><li>Use the correct child account and family group. Keep parent control passcodes separate from a child’s device-unlock code.</li><li>Review app approvals, purchase controls, age/content settings and time schedules together.</li><li>Configure each game or social platform’s own communication controls. A web filter is not an all-chat monitor.</li><li>Check recovery options and updates. On Apple devices using the same account, Share Across Devices can sync Screen Time settings and reports; verify each device.</li></ul><aside><p><strong>Use the instructions for the installed version.</strong> Apple’s current help and older iOS guides can show different menus. Screen Time does not have a general “VPN → Don’t Allow” switch. Supervised-device management is a different setup, not an ordinary Screen Time setting.</p></aside><h2>3. Choose one optional tool for one real gap</h2><p>Need cross-device rules and reports? Qustodio may be worth evaluating. Need supported content-risk alerts? Consider Bark’s exact app and OS coverage. Need explicit web-image filtering? Review Canopy’s supported surfaces. Compare these as alternatives, not a stack of simultaneous VPN-based filters.</p><p>On iOS, supported message analysis can use authorized desktop or device scans. Qustodio and Bark can analyze supported iMessage content through such workflows; this does not mean a router or VPN decrypts messages in transit. Collection can be delayed and depends on the scan setup being available.</p><p>Canopy’s supported saved-photo alerts are separate from its web filtering. They do not establish access to every private chat, encrypted folder or in-app image.</p><h2>4. Keep the router in its lane</h2><p>Existing router profiles and schedules may help with shared TVs, consoles and connected home devices. They generally cannot read HTTPS messages, enforce offline app use or follow a phone onto cellular without separate supported device controls. A new router is not the universal first purchase.</p><h2>5. Test the actual setup</h2><ul><li>Try a deliberately blocked app/site and an important allowed app on home Wi-Fi.</li><li>Repeat supported device checks on cellular or another network, and after a restart.</li><li>Check that reports reach the correct adult, and understand scan delays.</li><li>Verify product-specific removal settings. Ordinary profiles can be removable; extra protection has OS and enrollment limits.</li><li>Write down remaining gaps and revisit after major updates, new devices or changing family needs.</li></ul><p>No combination is bypass-proof. Keep the agreement understandable and age-appropriate, and make it safe for a child to ask for help.</p><p><a href="/stack-builder.html">Find a starting plan</a> or <a href="/solutions-comparison.html">compare current documented capabilities and prices</a>.</p>''', [('Apple: parental controls','https://support.apple.com/en-us/105121'),('Google: Family Link','https://families.google/familylink/'),('Microsoft: Family Safety setup','https://support.microsoft.com/en-us/family-safety/set-up-microsoft-family-safety'),('Qustodio: iOS calls and messages','https://help.qustodio.com/hc/en-us/articles/4986837688465-How-do-I-set-up-calls-messages-monitoring-for-iOS-devices'),('Bark: iOS monitoring setup','https://support.bark.us/en/articles/13461187-how-to-set-up-monitoring-on-iphone-and-ipad'),('Canopy: saved-photo scope','https://support.canopy.us/portal/en/kb/articles/change-sexting-alerts-level')])

article('blog/parental-controls-guide.html','Five parental-control checks worth making','You don’t need to change every setting tonight. Start with the account, then check what your child can install, access and share.','Parents / hands-on starting points','''<h2>1. Check the account and family group</h2><p>Use a child account with the correct age and supported family supervision. On Apple, review Family Sharing and Screen Time; enable Share Across Devices where appropriate and verify settings on each device. Protect Screen Time with a parent-only passcode.</p><p>Google Family Link supervision does not automatically end at 13. The applicable account age varies by country; Google’s current guidance says under-18s need parent approval to stop supervision. Follow the current account-transition instructions rather than removing controls based on a birthday alone.</p><h2>2. Make time limits intentional</h2><p>Choose downtime and app limits around your routine. Decide what stays available for school and contacting trusted people. Test the result on the child’s device. Microsoft Family Safety supports device limits on Windows/Xbox and supported app/game limits; the parent iOS app does not provide general child iOS enforcement.</p><h2>3. Review web and video settings separately</h2><p>Supervised Google SafeSearch is normally on and locked to Filter by default; verify it rather than assuming it was off. Signed-out sessions and other apps can have different coverage. Choose YouTube Kids or a supported supervised YouTube experience appropriate to age. YouTube Restricted Mode is not the same as a supervised experience.</p><p>Apple’s menu names vary by OS. Use the official parental-controls instructions for that version to review web content, app installation and purchase restrictions. Do not treat a time limit as a guaranteed app ban.</p><h2>4. Look inside games—not just at the router</h2><p>In Roblox, link a parent account or use supported Parent mode. Review content maturity, communication, spending and time settings. Restrict or disable supported chat as appropriate; do not weaken filtering as a safety step.</p><p>Communication eligibility, trusted-friend features and age checks vary. Some controls exclude creator-added chat, so test the actual experience. On consoles, use the manufacturer’s supported family controls; network blocking does not manage offline play or every in-game conversation.</p><h2>5. Review social communication together</h2><p>Instagram Teen Accounts include default safeguards. Linked supervision can show recent message contacts, not all message text; younger teens need permission for some less-protective settings. TikTok DMs are available only to registered users aged 16 or older; Family Pairing can restrict eligible teens’ settings.</p><p>Apple Communication Safety offers on-device protection in supported surfaces. It is not a dashboard of all third-party chats. Explain to children what you can see, what you cannot, and how they can ask for help without losing every privilege.</p><aside><p><strong>Finish with a real check.</strong> Try the important rules on home Wi-Fi and cellular, after a restart, and with every relevant account. Keep notes on what worked and what remains unsupported.</p></aside>''', [('Apple: parental controls by current OS','https://support.apple.com/en-us/105121'),('Apple: iOS 26 Screen Time guide','https://support.apple.com/guide/iphone/get-started-with-screen-time-iphbfa595995/26/ios/26'),('Google: age and supervision changes','https://support.google.com/families/answer/7106787?hl=en'),('Google: supervised SafeSearch','https://support.google.com/families/answer/7086922?hl=en'),('Roblox: parental controls overview','https://en.help.roblox.com/hc/en-us/articles/30428310121620-Parental-Controls-Overview'),('Meta: Instagram Teen Accounts','https://about.fb.com/news/2024/09/instagram-teen-accounts/'),('TikTok: Family Pairing','https://support.tiktok.com/en/safety-hc/account-and-user-safety/family-pairing'),('Apple: Communication Safety','https://support.apple.com/en-us/105069')])

article('blog/senior-scam-protection.html','A calmer way to handle suspicious calls and messages','Practical habits, safer account settings and a trusted-contact plan—with the older adult’s choices at the center.','Older adults / practical safety','''<h2>Start with a pause, not an app</h2><p>Scams often create urgency: a bank problem, an unexpected charge, a family emergency or someone claiming your computer is infected. A familiar voice, caller ID or convincing logo is not enough to verify the request.</p><p>End the interaction. Use a number already known to you, the number on your bank card or an independently opened official app. Do not use the link or callback number supplied by the suspicious message. Never share a verification code with an unexpected caller or pay someone demanding gift cards or cryptocurrency to fix a problem.</p><h2>Make important accounts easier to protect</h2><p>Review unique passwords, account recovery and explicitly enrolled two-step verification. A recovery phone number alone does not turn on two-factor authentication. Where supported and suitable, prefer a passkey or security key; keep recovery materials under the account owner’s control.</p><p>Gmail automatically filters spam. Optional Enhanced Safe Browsing adds phishing and malware checks—it is not a switch that starts basic spam filtering. Review suspicious forwarding rules and unfamiliar sessions with the account owner’s permission.</p><h2>Keep important calls coming through</h2><p>On supported iPhones, review Settings → Apps → Phone; screening and silencing options depend on the iOS version. Save doctors, family and other important callers first and practice reviewing voicemail. Silencing unknown callers can hide legitimate calls and does not identify every scam.</p><p>Google Call Screen is available only on supported devices and in supported regions and circumstances. Do not assume every Android phone has it. Ask your carrier about available spam controls and any separate fees.</p><h2>Make family involvement a choice</h2><p>An adult child paying for help does not authorize access to a parent’s private information. Agree directly on the devices, changes and information sharing. Use vendor invite-and-accept roles rather than shared credentials. Start with limited alerts and practice removing access.</p><p>Optional financial or identity monitoring can be useful for a specific need. It does not stop every payment or guarantee reimbursement. Free credit freezes can restrict access to a credit file for new credit; they are not the same as a paid credit lock, transaction alerts or protection against every kind of fraud.</p><h2>If something has already happened</h2><ol><li><strong>Contact the bank or payment provider immediately.</strong> Use a known number; describe the payment and ask about reversal or protective steps. Recovery is not guaranteed.</li><li><strong>Stop the suspicious interaction.</strong> If remote access may be active, disconnect the affected device from the internet and use a separate trusted device to contact providers. Avoid deleting unfamiliar tools or messages before considering evidence preservation.</li><li><strong>Secure affected accounts.</strong> Follow official recovery steps, review sessions and reset credentials from a trusted device as appropriate. Do not send passwords or codes to a helper.</li><li><strong>Keep records and report.</strong> Save relevant messages, receipts and dates. Use FTC reporting, IdentityTheft.gov for identity-theft recovery, and IC3 for internet-crime reports. Call emergency services if there is immediate physical danger.</li></ol><aside><p><strong>TechDad is not a 24/7 fraud-response service.</strong> Don’t wait for an assessment appointment to call your bank. Our free visit reviews priorities; remediation or setup is separate, quoted work.</p></aside>''', [('FTC: how to avoid a scam','https://consumer.ftc.gov/articles/how-avoid-scam'),('FTC: what to do if you were scammed','https://consumer.ftc.gov/articles/what-do-if-you-were-scammed'),('FTC: credit freezes and fraud alerts','https://consumer.ftc.gov/articles/what-know-about-credit-freezes-and-fraud-alerts'),('Report to the FTC','https://reportfraud.ftc.gov/'),('Identity theft recovery','https://www.identitytheft.gov/'),('FBI Internet Crime Complaint Center','https://www.ic3.gov/'),('Apple: unknown callers','https://support.apple.com/en-us/111106'),('Google: Call Screen availability','https://support.google.com/phoneapp/answer/9118387?hl=en'),('Google: 2-Step Verification','https://support.google.com/accounts/answer/185839?hl=en'),('Google: Enhanced Safe Browsing','https://support.google.com/accounts/answer/11577602?hl=en')],True)

article('how-it-works.html','How parental controls and safety tools work','Different tools address different risks. Knowing the boundaries is more useful than a row of green checkmarks.','Plain-English technical guide','''<h2>Native controls: rules on the device</h2><p>Screen Time, Family Link and Family Safety use supported platform capabilities to manage accounts, time and content. They can include activity reports, approval requests and selected notifications. Some local restrictions work offline; remote updates, location and reporting depend on connectivity and configuration.</p><p>They do not expose every message in every app. Choose by the child’s device and OS—not merely whether the parent can download a management app.</p><h2>Router controls: traffic on that network</h2><p>A router can schedule network access and filter supported connected traffic. DNS filters can block domains using different blocked-response methods; they generally cannot selectively read each HTTPS page, message or video. Encrypted DNS, VPNs and alternative networks can change visibility.</p><p>Home router settings do not control a phone on cellular or offline games. A separate supported mobile control may extend coverage; do not assume a bundled privacy VPN does that.</p><h2>Filtering apps: inspect only supported surfaces</h2><p>Products use different combinations of tunnels, DNS, content filtering and device management. A VPN alone does not decrypt HTTPS or end-to-end-encrypted conversations. Android certificate documentation is not proof of an identical iOS filtering mechanism.</p><p>Canopy’s web filtering and saved-photo sexting alerts are distinct features. Saved-photo scanning has permissions and folder limits; private/encrypted folders are not included. A VPN’s attributed data total may include other apps’ traffic, so it is not a measurement of extra cellular consumption.</p><h2>Message scans: authorized copies, not magic decryption</h2><p>Encryption in transit does not prevent an authorized tool from analyzing an available endpoint copy. Qustodio’s iOS Calls &amp; Messages app documents full SMS/iMessage content and supported WhatsApp/LINE concern alerts through a desktop-assisted workflow.</p><p>That setup needs compatible hardware/software, initial cable trust and a running desktop app. Automatic sync is hourly while the computer and child device share Wi-Fi. Bark also supports iOS content scans through supported methods; optional Bark Sync can work on Wi-Fi away from home. Analysis takes time and is not instantaneous.</p><p>App, OS, plan and collection method determine coverage. It is wrong to say no iOS message analysis is possible, and equally wrong to promise every app is monitored live.</p><h2>Removal resistance: verify the actual setup</h2><p>A configuration profile does not automatically mean the device is supervised or the profile is nonremovable. Stronger controls depend on enrollment and product-specific setup. Screen Time has no general VPN-toggle lock.</p><p>Qustodio’s reviewed Advance documentation supports iOS 15–26 and excludes iOS 27+. Canopy documents an additional iOS Removal Prevention setup; its default removal alerts do not themselves prevent removal. Check current compatibility before making changes.</p><h2>Why we don’t add up a “protection score”</h2><p>Overlapping consumer VPN-based filters can conflict. Different layers are not always on the same traffic path, and more subscriptions do not create additive safety. Start with native controls, select at most one optional paid filtering product for a real gap, then test your exact configuration.</p><p>A useful handoff says what was configured, what was tested, what may be delayed and what remains out of scope. It does not claim total protection.</p>''', [('Apple: parental controls','https://support.apple.com/en-us/105121'),('Apple: supervised MDM settings','https://support.apple.com/guide/deployment-education/common-mdm-settings-eduee8624eef/web'),('Qustodio: iOS message collection','https://help.qustodio.com/hc/en-us/articles/4986837688465-How-do-I-set-up-calls-messages-monitoring-for-iOS-devices'),('Qustodio: Advance OS compatibility','https://help.qustodio.com/hc/en-us/articles/20084558366482-How-to-prevent-Qustodio-from-being-removed-with-Qustodio-Advance-for-iOS'),('Bark: supported scan methods','https://support.bark.us/en/articles/13461187-how-to-set-up-monitoring-on-iphone-and-ipad'),('Canopy: removal options','https://support.canopy.us/portal/en/kb/articles/comparison-of-ios-removal-alerts-and-removal-prevention'),('Canopy: saved-photo limits','https://support.canopy.us/portal/en/kb/articles/change-sexting-alerts-level'),('Canopy: data and battery usage','https://support.canopy.us/portal/en/kb/articles/data-and-battery-usage')])

groups=[('native','Start with what’s built in'),('optional','Choose one optional paid tool'),('network','Review the router you already own'),('alternative','A conditional alternative'),('senior','Optional tools for adult needs')]
comparison=intro('Tools / reviewed November 25, 2026','A shorter list.<br>A clearer choice.','Choose for the gap you actually have. Built-in tools first, one compatible paid filter if needed—not every app at once.')
comparison+='<div class="wrap"><div class="comparison-intro"><p class="small">'+esc(DATA['basis'])+' These are vendor charges, not TechDad labor prices. No three-year totals or renewal estimates are implied where terms are unverified.</p><div class="anchor-nav">'+''.join(f'<a href="#{key}">{label}</a>' for key,label in groups)+'</div><a class="text-link" href="/stack-builder.html">Not sure where to begin? Try the plan finder →</a></div>'
for key,label in groups:
    comparison+=f'<section class="product-group" id="{key}"><h2>{label}</h2>'
    for p in DATA['products']:
        if p['group']!=key: continue
        comparison+=f'<details class="product"><summary><span>{esc(p["name"])}</span><span class="price">{esc(p["price"])}</span></summary><p><strong>Billing:</strong> {esc(p["billing"])}</p><p><strong>Device fit:</strong> {esc(p["devices"])}</p><p><strong>Useful for:</strong> {esc(p["coverage"])}</p><p><strong>Know the limits:</strong> {esc(p["limits"])}</p><div class="sources"><a href="{p["source"]}">Official product / pricing source</a>'+ (f' · <a href="{p["support"]}">Official support / terms</a>' if p.get('support') else '')+'</div></details>'
    comparison+='</section>'
comparison+='<div class="note"><p><strong>Existing Circle hardware?</strong> Confirm your exact generation and service before changing it. Current Circle pages promote Aura mobile controls; a new Home Plus hardware bundle, its price and Aura interoperability were not verified. That is not evidence that every existing Circle service has shut down.</p></div></div>'+cta()
page('solutions-comparison.html','Compare family safety tools, pricing and limitations','Source-linked comparison of native controls and optional filtering, router, identity and financial-alert tools. Dated billing and platform limitations.',comparison)

privacy='''<div class="wrap"><div class="page-intro"><span class="eyebrow">Privacy &amp; data use / November 25, 2026</span><h1>A little information.<br>A clear purpose.</h1><p class="lead">This notice describes how this website’s assessment request and optional analytics work.</p></div><article class="article" style="max-width:800px"><h2>Assessment requests</h2><p>The form asks for your name, email, town or neighborhood, who you want help for and a general main concern. Phone is optional. Those details go directly from your browser to our existing Formspark form endpoint at submit-form.com so TechDad CLT / AMP IT Solutions can respond and discuss scheduling.</p><p>Formspark processes submissions under its own service and privacy terms. Submission acceptance does not prove email delivery to our inbox. Your browser displays an error if it cannot confirm acceptance; it keeps your typed answers on that page so you can decide whether to retry.</p><p>Please do not send passwords, verification codes, financial records, identity documents, children’s names or sensitive incident details. We do not need them to discuss a visit. During agreed setup work, account owners enter their own credentials.</p><h2>What stays in your browser</h2><p>The plan finder runs locally and does not save or send your answers. The request form does not store a draft in browser storage. After a confirmed provider response, a short session marker lets the next page display an acknowledgment; it contains no form answers.</p><p>Your analytics preference is stored in local browser storage where available. This preference is specific to this browser and site. It can be changed using the controls in the footer.</p><h2>Optional site analytics</h2><p>This site retains its existing PostHog, Vercel Web Analytics and Vercel Speed Insights services to understand page use and performance, but loads them only after you allow analytics. They are disabled on the plan finder, intake and acknowledgment pages and on pages opened with a query string or fragment. We do not intentionally send form answers, plan-finder answers, names, email addresses or phone numbers as analytics events.</p><p>PostHog automatic click/form capture, session recordings and automatic page capture are disabled. The site sends a basic page-view event only; URL parameters and referrers are excluded from that event. Analytics providers still process technical request information under their own policies. Hosting and the form provider may process IP addresses and request metadata as part of serving the website or processing submissions, independently of optional analytics.</p><p>Turning analytics off reloads this page without optional analytics. Previous events cannot be recalled by changing the browser preference. If your browser signals Do Not Track or Global Privacy Control, this site leaves optional analytics off.</p><h2>Your choices and questions</h2><p>You may call or email instead of using the form. For a question about a request, or to ask about access, correction or deletion, email <a href="mailto:hello@ampitsolutions.com">hello@ampitsolutions.com</a>. We do not publish an unverified fixed retention period or promise deletion from every provider system here; handling depends on the records and provider requirements involved.</p><p>Arranging help for another adult does not authorize access to their private accounts or information. We discuss permission directly before changes and sharing.</p><h2>Provider information</h2><ul class="sources"><li><a href="https://formspark.io/privacy-policy/">Formspark privacy policy</a></li><li><a href="https://posthog.com/privacy">PostHog privacy policy</a></li><li><a href="https://vercel.com/legal/privacy-policy">Vercel privacy policy</a></li></ul></article></div>'''
page('privacy.html','Privacy and data use','How TechDad CLT’s assessment form, local plan finder and optional existing analytics process information.',privacy)

